# Security headers for the SPA, served by the Cloudflare Worker.
#
# Until this file existed the site answered with no security headers at all —
# no CSP, no HSTS, no nosniff, no framing rule. Verified against both
# deployments on 2026-08-19; the whole response was content-type,
# cache-control and server.
#
# `_headers` is read by Workers Static Assets from the assets directory
# (`./dist`, see wrangler.jsonc), so this file must be copied into the build
# output — Vite does that for everything in public/ automatically.
#
# Cloudflare applies *every* matching rule and appends the results, so two rules
# that both set the same header produce two headers rather than an override.
# That is why there is exactly one block here — see the Permissions-Policy note.

/*
  # Clickjacking. The checkout page renders Square's card iframe, and a
  # transparent overlay on top of somebody else's page is the classic way to
  # harvest a click on it. X-Frame-Options is the old spelling kept for
  # browsers that predate frame-ancestors; both say the same thing.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

  # MIME sniffing. Posters are user-uploaded into a public bucket, and while
  # they are served from the Supabase origin rather than this one, nosniff is
  # the cheap half of never letting a response be reinterpreted as script.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  # Ticket links carry the order token in the query string. Without this, that
  # token rides along in the Referer header to every third-party image, script
  # and outbound link the ticket page touches. strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # sends the bare origin off-site and the full URL only to ourselves.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # A year of HTTPS-only, including the apex and any future subdomain. Not
  # preloaded: preloading is effectively irreversible, and that is a decision
  # to make deliberately once the production hostname is settled, not a side
  # effect of an audit.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

  # One rule for the whole site, deliberately — there is no /admin override.
  #
  # The first shape of this was `camera=()` here and `camera=(self)` under
  # /admin/*, which reads correctly and does not work: Cloudflare *appends*
  # matching rules rather than replacing them, so /admin/scanner came back with
  # two Permissions-Policy headers. Confirmed on the staging deploy:
  #
  #   permissions-policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(self)
  #   permissions-policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(self)
  #
  # A browser resolving two of these does not take the more permissive one, so
  # the door scanner would have lost the camera — an outage on the one surface
  # that has to work with a queue in front of it, caused by a header nobody
  # would think to check.
  #
  # `camera=(self)` sitewide is the honest version: it permits same-origin
  # documents to *ask*, which is all the scanner needs and still leaves the
  # browser's own permission prompt in front of it. Cross-origin frames are
  # refused either way, which is the part that was ever worth having.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(self)

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Content-Security-Policy — ENFORCING since 2026-09-03
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # It shipped report-only on 2026-08-19, and that was the right call: driving a
  # real checkout under the first policy reported seven violations the card form
  # would have suffered — `card-wrapper.css` blocked by style-src-elem, and six
  # typefaces from three hosts blocked by font-src, one of them a cloudfront.net
  # distribution that appears in no documentation and was only discoverable by
  # watching the SDK ask for it.
  #
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  # What had to be settled before enforcing
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `index.html` carries an inline <script> — the boot watchdog added in #149,
  # which recovers the black empty page when the entry chunk 404s behind a stale
  # service worker. It MUST stay inline: it is the only code that still runs when
  # the module script fails to load, so an external file would fail in exactly
  # the case it exists for.
  #
  # script-src here has no 'unsafe-inline', so enforcing without provision would
  # have blocked it — silently disabling the recovery path for the failure it was
  # written to catch. It is allowed by hash instead, which keeps the policy
  # strict: a hash permits that exact script and nothing else, where
  # 'unsafe-inline' would permit any injected one and give up most of what a CSP
  # is for.
  #
  # Two things follow, and both are guarded by
  # src/lib/cspHashes.test.ts rather than by anyone remembering:
  #
  #   * The watchdog hash is stable across environments — it contains no
  #     %SITE_URL% token, so staging and production build byte-identical.
  #   * The JSON-LD block does NOT: it embeds the site URL, so it hashes
  #     differently per environment. Both are listed.
  #
  # The JSON-LD hashes are belt-and-braces. A <script type="application/ld+json">
  # is a data block, never "prepared" as a script, so script-src should not apply
  # to it at all — but that is a spec argument, and being wrong about it would
  # cost the structured data. Listing them is two tokens.
  #
  # To recompute after editing index.html: npm run build:production, then hash
  # the bytes between each <script> and </script>:
  #   node -e 'const c=require("crypto"),f=require("fs");
  #     [...f.readFileSync("dist/index.html","utf8").matchAll(/<script([^>]*)>([\s\S]*?)<\/script>/g)]
  #       .filter(m=>!/\bsrc=/.test(m[1]))
  #       .forEach(m=>console.log("sha256-"+c.createHash("sha256").update(m[2]).digest("base64")))'
  #
  # If a violation ever needs chasing, flip the header name back to
  # Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only and reproduce it in a browser console —
  # there is no report-uri, so violations are visible per-visitor and collected
  # nowhere.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-xpaI01Q6oUz6D4TVL7O0etHBvDbjrV8O36bKmJAXgrs=' 'sha256-8XoZJHvkL1iU1DBGeTJuQhjk3L96D4sjPMd4KwDkRes=' 'sha256-m8X3SWWViucQFG3j6LLrqGOltJxEgZnL/QZqziA+e64=' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://web.squarecdn.com https://sandbox.web.squarecdn.com https://*.squarecdn.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://*.squarecdn.com; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com https://*.squarecdn.com https://d1g145x70srn7h.cloudfront.net; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://*.supabase.co wss://*.supabase.co https://pci-connect.squareup.com https://pci-connect.squareupsandbox.com https://connect.squareup.com https://connect.squareupsandbox.com https://*.squarecdn.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://*.squarecdn.com https://connect.squareup.com https://connect.squareupsandbox.com https://www.youtube.com https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

